🌊 MODEL 1 — OIL SPILL DETECTION & SEGMENTATION
Dataset

Uses the Zenodo Sentinel-1 SAR dataset, divided into:

Oil-spill samples — SAR images with oil-spill masks
Negative samples — no-oil and look-alike patterns
Test data — unseen oil, no-oil and look-alike images
Processing

Sentinel-1 SAR → VV + VH → Separate P2–P98 Normalization → 512×512 Tiles

VV and VH are normalized separately to preserve their different backscatter distributions and reduce the effect of extreme values.

AI Architecture

ResNet-34 Encoder + U-Net Decoder

Input: 512 × 512 × 2 (VV + VH)
Pretrained ResNet-34 extracts spatial features.
U-Net decoder reconstructs the spill boundaries using upsampling and skip connections.
Output: 512 × 512 oil-spill probability mask
Geospatial Output

Binary Mask → GeoJSON Polygon → Spill Area + Centroid (Lat/Lon)

Purpose: Accurately identify where the oil spill is and its extent.

🌊 MODEL 2 — SPILL ORIGIN & DRIFT PREDICTION
Input

Receives Model 1 outputs:

Oil-spill polygon
Centroid coordinates
Spill area
Satellite observation time
Environmental Data

Combines:

Ocean currents — Copernicus Marine
Wind — ERA5 10-metre wind
Waves & Stokes drift — Copernicus Marine

Only the required location and time window around the detected spill is processed.

Particle-Based Drift Model

Creates 500–1,000 virtual particles inside the detected spill.

Total movement = Ocean Current + Windage + Stokes Drift + Diffusion

Uses RK4 integration with spatial and temporal interpolation to simulate particle movement.

Two-Way Prediction
Backward tracking: Estimates the probable origin and time.
Forward tracking: Predicts the 6–72 hour future drift and spread.
Output

Origin region + origin time window + predicted drift paths + probability/uncertainty map + coastal-risk alerts

Purpose: Understand where the spill came from and where it may go next.

🚢 MODEL 3 — VESSEL ATTRIBUTION & SUSPECT RANKING
Input

Uses Model 2 outputs:

Probable origin region
Origin time window
Backward drift path
Uncertainty radius
AIS Data

Uses historical AIS data containing MMSI, timestamp, latitude, longitude, SOG, COG and vessel information.

Processing

AIS Data → Cleaning → MMSI Grouping → Trajectory Reconstruction → Spatial & Temporal Filtering

Removes irrelevant vessels and identifies vessels that were near the probable origin during the relevant time window.

Attribution Features

Evaluates:

Distance from origin
Time match
Trajectory overlap
Speed/course changes
AIS transmission gaps
Vessel type
Output

Generates a transparent 0–100 suspect score and ranked vessel list with the reason for each ranking.

Purpose: Identify which vessels were potentially associated with the spill, based on measurable evidence—not as proof of responsibility.
